--- PLATFORM-CAPABILITY NOTE (UI-automation E2E suite) ---
The full-solution `dotnet test apps/windows/TeslaSync.sln` reports TEST_EXIT=1 SOLELY because of 147 tests in
TeslaSync.App.UITests (every one tagged [Trait("Category","UIAutomation")]). They fail — before this surface
existed and unrelated to it — with:
  TeslaSync.App.UITests.Fixtures.UiAutomationUnavailableException : UI automation cannot run:
  WinAppDriver/Appium runner is absent: nothing is listening on http://127.0.0.1:4723/ and
  WinAppDriver.exe was not found at its standard install paths or on PATH.
  at WinAppDriverSession.EnsureReady() ... WinAppDriverSession.cs:line 75
Per-assembly result of the full-solution run:
  Failed! - Failed: 147, Passed:     0 - TeslaSync.App.UITests.dll   (WinAppDriver runner absent)
  Passed! - Failed:   0, Passed: 12211 - TeslaSync.App.Tests.dll     (this surface's headless gate)
That end-to-end UI-automation suite is gated SEPARATELY by apps/windows/run-ui-automation.ps1 (P2/W9-0002),
which by design exits STATUS=BLOCKED when the runner is absent and "never reports an absent runner as green"
(WinAppDriverSession fails loud rather than silently skipping). It exercises shell-level flows (navigation,
command palette, auth, platform activation) — NOT this feature view — and requires WinAppDriver 1.2.1+,
Developer Mode and a deployed packaged app, none of which exist on this headless runner. This is a runner
capability gap, NOT a defect in the AutomationCard surface: the surface builds, and every one of its required
headless tests passes. Same accepted precedent as the sibling AlertCard (0179) and LiveVehicleState (0044)
surface logs.

